      { "question": "Is it normal for a child to complain about practicing violin?", "answer": "Some complaining can happen even when a child genuinely cares about violin — repetition and difficulty are part of learning any instrument. What matters more than an occasional complaint is whether the overall pattern, over weeks rather than days, still includes curiosity and engagement." },
      { "question": "Can a child enjoy violin and still get frustrated?", "answer": "Yes. Challenge and enjoyment aren't opposites, and frustration on its own doesn't mean a child dislikes what they're doing. What's worth noticing is whether the frustration stays manageable and whether your child tends to recover from it, especially with a smaller step or a different explanation." },
      { "question": "How can I tell if my child wants to quit violin?", "answer": "One difficult day rarely answers that question. Look for a sustained pattern across several weeks — across lessons, practice, and repertoire — rather than deciding based on a single rough session." },
      { "question": "What should I do if my child says they don't like violin?", "answer": "Start with curiosity instead of immediately deciding to push through or to stop. Try to understand what specifically isn't working, and loop in the teacher, who often sees a different side of your child than you do at home." },
      { "question": "Should parents make children practice violin?", "answer": "There's no single right answer. Age-appropriate structure and reminders can help a child build a routine, while leaving room for real choices — what to play, how to order an assignment — tends to support engagement better than constant enforcement." },
      { "question": "Can changing the music help a child enjoy violin more?", "answer": "For some students, yes. Repertoire that connects to a child's actual musical taste can meaningfully affect motivation, even while foundational technique stays part of the plan. It's worth raising with your child's teacher if repertoire seems to be part of what's not clicking." }
